---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Button from "../../components/Button.astro";
import Stat from "../../components/stat.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Matt Rabah is a Detroit-based CX, customer success, and implementation professional focused on customer lifecycle systems, CRM discipline, and practical AI workflows."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal reveal-1">About</p>
      <h1 class="display reveal reveal-2">Matt Rabah.</h1>
      <p
        class="lead reveal reveal-3"
        style="max-width:50ch; margin-top:var(--space-6)"
      >
        I'm a Detroit-based customer experience and implementation professional.
        I've worked across clinical platforms, luxury retail brands, and
        independent projects at Cadence Lab.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="stat-row">
        <Stat value="7+" label="Years in CX & CS" />
        <Stat
          value="3"
          label="Industries"
          note="Healthtech · Retail · Consulting"
        />
        <Stat
          value="M.S."
          label="CX Management"
          note="Michigan State · Eli Broad"
        />
        <Stat
          value="Salesforce"
          label="Certified Admin"
          note="Platform Administrator"
          compact
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p>
        I've spent the better part of a decade across{" "}
        <strong>Cadence Lab, Shinola, and NeuroSync</strong>. Different
        industries, yet the same thread runs through all of it: customers feel
        the operating model. They feel the handoff, the missed note, the unclear
        owner, the delayed follow-up, and the moment a team has to rediscover
        what should already have been documented.
      </p>

      <p>
        I've worked across healthcare accounts, luxury retail customer
        experience, portfolio growth, Salesforce process, lifecycle diagnostics,
        onboarding, and retention systems. The job has never really been "manage
        relationships." It's been making sure the relationship doesn't depend on
        one person remembering everything.
      </p>

      <p>
        I hold an{" "}
        <strong>master's degree in Customer Experience Management</strong> from
        Michigan State University's Eli Broad College of Business and before
        that, I earned my bachelor's degree in Organizational Behavior from
        Oakland University. I'm a Salesforce Certified Agentforce Specialist,
        with additional work in product management and building AI within the
        enterprise.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Right now</p>
      <h2 class="h2" style="margin:var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-5)">
        Exploring AI in customer operations.
      </h2>
      <p>
        My current focus is AI inside customer-facing operations, not as a
        novelty layer, but as a way to cut administrative drag, prep better, and
        surface risk earlier without blurring who owns what. Healthtech and
        other regulated environments are a natural fit, because trust is fragile
        there and implementation discipline actually matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm <strong>open to roles</strong> in customer success, CX,
        implementation, and RevOps-adjacent work. If that's what you're
        building, I'd like to hear about it.
      </p>
      <div style="margin-top:var(--space-6); display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:1rem">
        <Button href="/resume" variant="ghost" arrow>
          View resume
        </Button>
        <Button href="/contact" variant="accent" arrow>
          Start a conversation
        </Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
